rain\",\n      \"Autonomic nervous system\",\n      \"Spinal cord\",\n      \"Peripheral nervous system\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Most reflex actions are mediated primarily by the spinal cord without conscious involvement from the brain, ensuring rapid responses.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 51,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Inborn reflexes are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Conditional reflexes\",\n      \"Unconditional reflexes\",\n      \"Modified reflexes\",\n      \"All\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Inborn, natural reflexes that do not require prior learning are termed unconditional reflexes.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 52,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cycling is an example of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Conditional reflex\",\n      \"Unconditional reflex\",\n      \"Inborn reflex\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Cycling is an acquired skill that involves learning and practice, classifying it as a conditional reflex.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 53,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Pioneer work on conditional reflex was done by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Karmer\",\n      \"Pavlov\",\n      \"Darwin\",\n      \"Oparin\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Ivan Pavlov pioneered the study of conditional reflexes through his classic experiments with dogs.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 54,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The nervous pathway taken by nerve impulses in reflex action is called\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Reflex flow\",\n      \"Neuro muscular path\",\n      \"Reflex arc\",\n      \"Neural arc\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A reflex arc is the neural pathway that controls a reflex, spanning from the sensory receptor to the effector muscle or gland.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 55,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"A simple reflex arc has the following sequence\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sensory neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ receptor $\\\\rightarrow$ interneuron $\\\\rightarrow$ motor neuron\",\n      \"Sensory neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ motor neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ receptor\",\n      \"Receptor $\\\\rightarrow$ motor neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ interneuron $\\\\rightarrow$ sensory neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ effector\",\n      \"Receptor $\\\\rightarrow$ sensory neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ interneuron $\\\\rightarrow$ motor neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ effector\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The correct sequential pathway of a reflex arc begins with a receptor, travels along a sensory neuron, connects through an interneuron, proceeds down a motor neuron, and ends at an effector.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 56,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"In Pavlov experiment the sound of bell represents\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Conditional stimulus\",\n      \"Unconditional response\",\n      \"Unconditional stimulus\",\n      \"Conditional response\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"In Pavlov's experiments, the previously neutral sound of the bell became a conditional stimulus after being repeatedly paired with food.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 57,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"In spinal cord, the distribution of grey matter and white matter is as\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Grey matter outside and white matter at centre\",\n      \"White matter outside and grey matter at centre\",\n      \"Grey matter at anterior and white matter at posterior side\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"In the spinal cord, the white matter is situated on the periphery, while the grey matter is centrally located, often forming an H-shape.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 58,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The central canal inside the spinal cord is filled with\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Plasma\",\n      \"Serum\",\n      \"Lymph\",\n      \"Cerebrospinal fluid\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The central canal runs through the spinal cord and contains cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), continuous with the brain's ventricles.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 59,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Space present inside the vertebra for passage of spinal cord is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Neurocoel\",\n      \"Neural canal\",\n      \"Central canal\",\n      \"Nerve cord\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vertebral foramen or neural canal is the bony opening inside the vertebra that accommodates and protects the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 60,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The tapering end of spinal cord is known as\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Urostyle\",\n      \"Filum terminale\",\n      \"Connus medularis\",\n      \"Cauda equina\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The conus medullaris (or connus medularis) is the tapered, lower end of the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 61,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The method of taking out CSF from the spinal subarachnoid space between L3 and L4 is called\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Lumbar puncture\",\n      \"Spinal puncture\",\n      \"CSF puncture\",\n      \"Neural puncture\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A lumbar puncture (spinal tap) is the procedure of inserting a needle between the $L_3$ and $L_4$ vertebrae to sample CSF.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 62,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Dorsal root of spinal cord is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Afferent and motor\",\n      \"Afferent and sensory\",\n      \"Efferent and motor\",\n      \"Efferent and sensory\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The dorsal root carries sensory (afferent) nerve fibers that transmit information into the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 63,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Neuromuscular junction is found between\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Neuron and bone\",\n      \"Two neurons\",\n      \"Neuron and muscle\",\n      \"Neuron and nerves\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A neuromuscular junction is the synapse connecting a motor neuron and a muscle fiber.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 64,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Synapse is the bringing together of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Two nerve fibres\",\n      \"Two veins\",\n      \"Two neurons\",\n      \"Two axons\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A synapse functionally connects two neurons, permitting signals to pass from the presynaptic to the postsynaptic neuron.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 65,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The white matter of the brain is formed by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Axons only\",\n      \"Cell bodies of neurons only\",\n      \"Axons and dendrites\",\n      \"All axons, dendrites & cell bodies\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"White matter predominantly consists of myelinated axons and dendrites, giving it a characteristic pale appearance.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 66,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Central nervous system consists of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Brain\",\n      \"Brain and spinal cord only\",\n      \"Brain and cranial nerves\",\n      \"Spinal cord only\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The Central Nervous System (CNS) is strictly comprised of the brain and the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 67,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The peripheral nervous system includes the nerves arising from\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Brain\",\n      \"Spinal cord\",\n      \"Central neural system\",\n      \"Autonomous nervous system\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The peripheral nervous system consists of all the nerves branching out from the central neural system (brain and spinal cord).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 68,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Autonomic nervous system of rabbit includes\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cranial and spinal nerves\",\n      \"Brain and spinal cord\",\n      \"Sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves\",\n      \"Medullated and non medullated nerves\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"In mammals, including rabbits, the autonomic nervous system is divided into sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 69,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cranial and spinal nerves are part of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Central nervous system\",\n      \"Peripheral nervous system\",\n      \"Sympathetic nervous system\",\n      \"Parasympathetic nervous system\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Cranial nerves from the brain and spinal nerves from the spinal cord collectively constitute the peripheral nervous system.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 70,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Autonomic nervous system controls\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sense organs\",\n      \"Internal organs\",\n      \"Reflex action\",\n      \"Skeletal muscles\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The autonomic nervous system involuntarily regulates internal organs, smooth muscles, and glands.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 71,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"If the sympathetic nerve to heart is cut on at normal condition heart beat will\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Increase\",\n      \"Decrease\",\n      \"Remains same\",\n      \"Not affected\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Under normal basal conditions, the sympathetic nerve lacks significant tonic influence, so cutting it leaves the resting heart rate largely unchanged.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 72,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"If parasympathetic nerve to heart is cut off heart beat will\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Increase\",\n      \"Decrease\",\n      \"Heart beats without nervous control\",\n      \"Heart beat stops\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Although cutting the parasympathetic tone increases heart rate, the immediate result is the intrinsic pacemaker taking over without the vagal braking, leaving the heart to beat devoid of extrinsic parasympathetic nervous control.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 73,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"If the vagus nerve going to heart is cut off, heart beat will\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Decrease\",\n      \"Increase\",\n      \"Remain same\",\n      \"Not affected\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vagus nerve continuously exerts an inhibitory (parasympathetic) tone on the heart; severing it releases this inhibition, leading to an increased heart rate.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 74,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which system prepares the body for acute or stressful or emergency condition?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Central nervous system\",\n      \"Sympathetic nervous system\",\n      \"Parasympathetic nervous system\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The sympathetic nervous system orchestrates the 'fight or flight' response, preparing the body for stressful or emergency situations.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 75,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Sympathetic nervous system is a\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Craniosacral outflow\",\n      \"Thoracolumbar outflow\",\n      \"Sacral outflow\",\n      \"Cholinergic system\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The sympathetic nervous system originates from the thoracic and lumbar regions of the spinal cord, hence it is termed the thoracolumbar outflow.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 76,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The common neurotransmitter of parasympathetic nervous system is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Adrenaline\",\n      \"Nor adrenaline\",\n      \"Both a & b\",\n      \"Acetylcholine\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Acetylcholine is the primary neurotransmitter released by parasympathetic postganglionic neurons.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 77,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following neurotransmitter is involved in increasing the heart beat?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Dopamine\",\n      \"Nor adrenaline\",\n      \"Acetylcholine\",\n      \"GTP\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Noradrenaline (norepinephrine), released by sympathetic fibers, acts on the heart's pacemaker cells to accelerate the heart rate.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 78,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Neurotransmitter between neuron and muscle cell or neuromuscular junction is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Dopamine\",\n      \"Serotonin\",\n      \"Acetylcholine\",\n      \"GTP\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Acetylcholine is the chemical transmitter released by motor neurons at the neuromuscular junction to stimulate muscle contraction.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 79,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Touch on the right side stimulates neurons on\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Right somatic sensory area\",\n      \"Right somatic motor area\",\n      \"Left somatic sensory area\",\n      \"Left somatic motor area\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Due to decussation (crossing over) of nerve tracts, sensory input from the right side of the body is processed by the left somatic sensory area of the brain.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 80,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Number of cranial nerves found in rabbit is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$12$\",\n      \"$10$\",\n      \"$24$\",\n      \"$20$\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Like humans, rabbits have $12$ pairs of cranial nerves, making a total of $24$ individual cranial nerves.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 81,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Number of spinal nerves in human is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$12$ pairs\",\n      \"$37$ pairs\",\n      \"$31$ pairs\",\n      \"none\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Humans possess $31$ pairs of spinal nerves originating from the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 82,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Total number of nerves arising from CNS in rabbit is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$12$ pairs\",\n      \"$37$ pairs\",\n      \"$31$ pairs\",\n      \"$49$ pairs\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A rabbit has $12$ pairs of cranial nerves and $37$ pairs of spinal nerves, bringing the total to $49$ pairs arising from the CNS.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 83,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"All spinal nerves are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Mixed cranial nerves\",\n      \"Purely sensory\",\n      \"Purely motor\",\n      \"Mixed\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Spinal nerves are formed by the fusion of dorsal (sensory) and ventral (motor) roots, rendering them entirely mixed nerves.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 84,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cranial nerves are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Mixed\",\n      \"Purely motor\",\n      \"Purely sensory\",\n      \"All\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Cranial nerves vary in function: some are purely sensory, some are purely motor, and others are mixed.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 85,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which cranial nerve is purely sensory?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Abducens\",\n      \"Auditory\",\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Trochlear\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The auditory (vestibulocochlear) nerve is cranial nerve VIII and functions purely for sensory input (hearing and balance).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 86,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Purely sensory cranial nerves are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$1, 2$ and $6$\",\n      \"$5, 7, 9, 10$\",\n      \"$1, 2$ and $8$\",\n      \"$1$ and $2$\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Cranial nerves I (Olfactory), II (Optic), and VIII (Auditory) are strictly sensory in function.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 87,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Shortest cranial nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Olfactory\",\n      \"Optic\",\n      \"Trochlear\",\n      \"Vagus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Although olfactory is often debated as the shortest, some specific textbook references designate the optic nerve due to its immediate entry into the chiasm.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 88,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Pneumogastric nerve is also known as\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Glossopharyngeal\",\n      \"Spinal accessory\",\n      \"Auditory\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) is historically known as the pneumogastric nerve due to its extensive innervation of the lungs and stomach.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 89,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The smallest and thinnest cranial nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Trochlear\",\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Facial\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The trochlear nerve (cranial nerve IV) contains the fewest axons and is considered the smallest and thinnest cranial nerve.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 90,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"A cranial nerve with maximum branches in the body is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Auditory\",\n      \"Facial\",\n      \"Abducens\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vagus nerve has the most extensive distribution, branching out to innervate almost all organs in the thoracic and abdominal cavities.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 91,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following has longest intracranial course?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Abducens\",\n      \"Facial\",\n      \"Optic\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The abducens nerve (CN VI) has the longest pathway within the skull cavity (intracranial course).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 92,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Longest cranial nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Abducens\",\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Hypoglossal\",\n      \"Auditory\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vagus nerve is the longest cranial nerve because it extends far beyond the head and neck to the thoracic and abdominal organs.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 93,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"First spinal nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Olfactory\",\n      \"Hypoglossal\",\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Mixed\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Like all spinal nerves, the first spinal nerve ($C_1$) carries both sensory and motor fibers, hence it is a mixed nerve.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 94,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Fifth cranial nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Optic\",\n      \"Trochlear\",\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Facial\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The fifth cranial nerve (CN V) is the trigeminal nerve, serving primarily sensory functions in the face.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 95,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Odd one is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Optic\",\n      \"Occulomotor\",\n      \"Olfactory\",\n      \"Auditory\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Optic, olfactory, and auditory are purely sensory nerves, whereas the occulomotor nerve primarily has motor functions.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 96,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which are purely motor nerves?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$3^{\\\\text{rd}}, 4^{\\\\text{th}}, 6^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"$9^{\\\\text{th}}, 10^{\\\\text{th}}, 12^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"$11^{\\\\text{th}}, 12^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"Both a & c\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Cranial nerves III, IV, VI, XI, and XII are primarily motor nerves, meaning both sets a and c are correct.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 97,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Motor cranial nerve is/are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Optic\",\n      \"Occulomotor\",\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Both b & c\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Although trigeminal is mixed, it possesses a significant motor component for mastication; occulomotor is predominantly motor.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 98,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Vagus nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Purely sensory\",\n      \"Purely motor\",\n      \"Parasympathetic\",\n      \"Sympathetic\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vagus nerve is the primary parasympathetic nerve of the body, providing major parasympathetic input to visceral organs.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 99,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The cranial nerves carrying parasympathetic outflow are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$5, 6, 7, 8$\",\n      \"$2, 5, 7, 12$\",\n      \"$3, 7, 9, 10$\",\n      \"$1, 2, 3, 4$\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Parasympathetic fibers travel within the cranial nerves III (oculomotor), VII (facial), IX (glossopharyngeal), and X (vagus).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 100,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Mixed cranial nerve are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$5, 6, 7, 8$\",\n      \"$3, 7, 9, 10$\",\n      \"$5, 7, 9, 10$\",\n      \"$1, 2, 3, 4$\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Cranial nerves V (trigeminal), VII (facial), IX (glossopharyngeal), and X (vagus) contain both sensory and motor fibers, hence they are mixed.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 101,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Fore brain of human/rabbit does not contain\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Olfactory lobes\",\n      \"Crura cerebri\",\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Diencephalon\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The crura cerebri are structures located in the midbrain, not the forebrain.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 102,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Gyri and Sulci are the features of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Gyri (ridges) and sulci (grooves) are prominent characteristic folds on the surface of the cerebral cortex.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 103,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following is the part of midbrain of rabbit?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Diencephalon\",\n      \"Corpora quadrigemina\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The corpora quadrigemina (superior and inferior colliculi) are key structures of the midbrain tectum.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 104,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which is not a part of hindbrain?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Thalamus\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Pons\",\n      \"Medulla\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The thalamus is a component of the diencephalon, which belongs to the forebrain. The hindbrain consists of the pons, cerebellum, and medulla.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 105,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Betz cells and pyramidal cells are the characteristic of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Pons varolli\",\n      \"Crura cerebri\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Betz cells and pyramidal cells are distinctive large neurons located in the motor cortex of the cerebrum.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 106,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following is also called 'little brain'?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The term cerebellum translates directly from Latin as 'little brain' and coordinates motor activities.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 107,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The two cavity present in cerebral hemispheres are called\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Rhinocoel\",\n      \"Diocoel\",\n      \"Ventricles\",\n      \"Lateral ventricles\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The right and left lateral ventricles are the fluid-filled cavities located within each cerebral hemisphere.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 108,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The third ventricle is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Diencephalon\",\n      \"Metacoel\",\n      \"Parocoel\",\n      \"Diocoel\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The third ventricle is anatomically located within the diencephalon and is also known as the diocoel.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 109,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Lateral ventricles are connected to the third ventricle by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Foramen Magnum\",\n      \"Foramina Luschka\",\n      \"Foramen of Monro\",\n      \"Foramen of Iter\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The interventricular foramina, commonly known as the foramina of Monro, connect the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 110,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cavity of medulla oblongata is known as\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Rhinocoel\",\n      \"Metacoel\",\n      \"Optocoel\",\n      \"Diacoel\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The cavity extending into the medulla oblongata is traditionally termed the metacoel (part of the fourth ventricle).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 111,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Brain stem does not contain\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Midbrain\",\n      \"Pons\",\n      \"Medulla\",\n      \"Cerebellum\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The brain stem is composed of the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata; the cerebellum is situated behind it and is not part of the brain stem itself.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 112,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Alcohol effects\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"Cerebral cortex\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Alcohol consumption highly affects the cerebellum, impairing balance and voluntary motor coordination.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 113,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Centre of thirst, sex and hunger is located in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The hypothalamus contains crucial nuclei that regulate basic autonomic drives, including hunger, thirst, and sexual behavior.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 114,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The main centre for autonomic nervous response is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"Cerebrum\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The hypothalamus acts as the major integrating center for the autonomic nervous system and endocrine responses.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 115,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Centre for primary visual memory lies in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Cerebral cortex\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"Pons varolli\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Visual processing and visual memory are primarily associated with the cerebral cortex, especially within the occipital and temporal lobes.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 116,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Structure connected with vision in rabbit is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Corpus callosum\",\n      \"Corpora quadrigemina\",\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"In rabbits (and mammals), the superior colliculi of the corpora quadrigemina are involved in visual reflexes.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 117,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Visual area lies in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Frontal lobe\",\n      \"Parietal lobe\",\n      \"Temporal lobe\",\n      \"Occipital lobe\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The primary visual cortex is located at the very back of the brain within the occipital lobe.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 118,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Voluntary muscular coordination & equilibrium is maintained by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The cerebellum receives motor commands and sensory feedback to coordinate smooth voluntary muscle movements and maintain posture and equilibrium.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 119,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Main function of cerebellum is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Balancing\",\n      \"Sight\",\n      \"Hearing\",\n      \"Remembering\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The primary and most notable function of the cerebellum is to regulate motor control, balance, and posture.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 120,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Centre for sensation of smell and sound in located in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Frontal lobe\",\n      \"Parietal lobe\",\n      \"Temporal lobe\",\n      \"Occipital lobe\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The temporal lobe houses the primary auditory cortex and is heavily involved in processing sound as well as olfactory signals.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 121,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"General sensory area like heat, pain, cold, touch is located\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Frontal lobe\",\n      \"Parietal lobe\",\n      \"Temporal lobe\",\n      \"Occipital lobe\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The somatosensory cortex, which processes somatic sensations like touch, pain, temperature, and proprioception, is located in the parietal lobe.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 122,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Vomiting, coughing, sneezing and swallowing centre lies in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The medulla oblongata controls many vital autonomic reflexes, including vomiting, coughing, swallowing, and sneezing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 123,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Nerve related to diaphragm is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Phrenic\",\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Glossopharyngeal\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The phrenic nerve is the specific motor nerve that innervates the diaphragm, enabling breathing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 124,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Pons varolli connects\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Brain with spinal cord\",\n      \"Cerebrum with cerebellum\",\n      \"Two-lobes of cerebellum\",\n      \"Two cerebral hemispheres\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The pons serves as a major relay center connecting different parts of the brain, including linking upper brain regions to the medulla and spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 125,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"CSF continues as spinal cord from brain through\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Foramen of Monoro\",\n      \"Obturator foramen\",\n      \"Foramen Magnum\",\n      \"Aqueduct of Sylvius\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The Foramen Magnum is the large opening at the base of the skull where the brainstem transitions into the spinal cord, allowing CSF and tissues to pass.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 126,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Brain and spinal cord are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Receptors\",\n      \"Intermediatry neurons\",\n      \"Neurosecretory\",\n      \"Nervous organs\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The brain and spinal cord are the principal macroscopic nervous organs comprising the central nervous system.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 127,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Heart beat is decreased by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sympathetic system\",\n      \"Parasympathetic system\",\n      \"Reflex arc\",\n      \"Central neural system\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The parasympathetic nervous system (specifically via the vagus nerve) slows down the intrinsic heart rate.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 128,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Neurons producing hormone like substance are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Neurosecretory\",\n      \"Sensory\",\n      \"Motor\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Neurosecretory cells (such as those in the hypothalamus) synthesize and release hormones directly into the bloodstream.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 129,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which has H-shaped gray matter?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Spinal cord\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Brain\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A transverse section of the spinal cord characteristically reveals a central core of grey matter arranged in the shape of a butterfly or the letter 'H'.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 130,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Dorsal horn and ventral horn are related to\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Brain\",\n      \"Spinal cord\",\n      \"Cranial nerves\",\n      \"Synapsis\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The dorsal and ventral horns represent regions of the central grey matter within the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 131,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The ventral root of spinal cord carries the\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Motor neurons carrying the message away from spinal cord\",\n      \"Motor neurons carrying the message towards the spinal cord\",\n      \"Sensory neurons carrying the message away from spinal cord\",\n      \"Sensory neurons carrying the message towards the spinal cord\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Ventral roots contain the axons of motor (efferent) neurons which send impulses away from the spinal cord to target muscles and glands.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 132,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Largest part of the brain of man is called\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Olfactory lobe\",\n      \"Optic chiasma\",\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Corpus callosum\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The cerebrum comprises about 85% of the brain's total weight and is significantly the largest part of the human brain.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 133,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Memory, weakens if one of the following parts is injured\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Medulla\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Higher cognitive functions including learning, reasoning, and memory are principally localized within the cerebral cortex (cerebrum).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 134,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The cranial nerve which brings impulses from the internal ear is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Hypomadibular\",\n      \"Trochlear\",\n      \"Auditory\",\n      \"Vagus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The auditory nerve (vestibulocochlear nerve, CN VIII) transmits sensory data related to hearing and equilibrium from the internal ear to the brain.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 135,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of these processes is found in animal only?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Neural control\",\n      \"Hormonal control\",\n      \"Respiration\",\n      \"Diffusion\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"While both plants and animals possess chemical/hormonal control, respiration, and diffusion, only animals have an evolved nervous system for neural control.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 136,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following cranial nerves in man is both sensory and motor?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Optic\",\n      \"Olfactory\",\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Auditory\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) is a mixed nerve carrying significant sensory input from the face and motor output for chewing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 1,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which cells do not form the layer and remains structurally separate?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Epithelial cells\",\n      \"Gland cells\",\n      \"Muscle cells\",\n      \"Nerve cells\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Unlike epithelial or muscle cells which bind tightly to form distinct layered tissues, nerve cells generally exist as individually discrete interconnected units.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 2,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following neuron is generally absent in adult human being?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Multipolar neuron\",\n      \"Bipolar neuron\",\n      \"Pseudounipolar neuron\",\n      \"Unipolar neuron\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"True unipolar neurons are predominantly found in invertebrates and during embryonic development, but they are typically absent or rare in adult human beings (who instead have pseudounipolar neurons).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 3,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Pseudounipolar neurons occur in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Pyramidal cells of cerebral cortex\",\n      \"Retina of eye\",\n      \"Schneiderian membrane\",\n      \"Cells of dorsal root ganglion\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The primary sensory neurons situated within the dorsal root ganglia are classic examples of pseudounipolar neurons.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 4,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which one of the following glial cells form myelin sheath in PNS?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Astrocytes\",\n      \"Microglia cells\",\n      \"Oligodendrocytes\",\n      \"Neurolemmocyte\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Neurolemmocytes, another name for Schwann cells, are responsible for myelinating axons in the peripheral nervous system.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 5,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The largest number of cell bodies of neurons in our body is found in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Brain\",\n      \"Retina\",\n      \"Spinal cord\",\n      \"Tongue\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The brain houses roughly 86 billion neurons, containing the vast majority of neuron cell bodies in the human body.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 6,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Daily posture like standing and walking is controlled by which part of brain?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Frontal lobe\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Temporal lobe\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The cerebellum finely tunes motor movements, playing an indispensable role in maintaining balance, posture, and coordinated walking.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 7,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"An example of conditional reflex is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sneezing during cold\",\n      \"Running indoor on arrival of rain\",\n      \"Withdrawal of hand on touching a hot surface\",\n      \"Salivation of dog on listening the bell as found by Pavlov\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Pavlov's experiment where dogs learned to salivate to a bell's sound is the quintessential example of a learned or conditioned reflex.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 8,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Simple two-neuron reflex consists of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sensory neuron\",\n      \"Effector neuron\",\n      \"Both a & b\",\n      \"Sensory neuron, effector neuron and spinal cord\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A monosynaptic (two-neuron) reflex involves a sensory (afferent) neuron and a motor (effector) neuron synapsing within the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 9,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which one is not a reflex action?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Food swallowing\",\n      \"Closing of eye lids in bright light\",\n      \"Perspiration due to heat\",\n      \"Salivation on seeing sweets\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"While perspiration is an involuntary autonomic response to temperature (thermoregulation), it is generally considered a physiological homeostatic mechanism rather than a classical rapid reflex arc.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 10,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Breast feeding by newly born child is an example of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Unconditional reflex\",\n      \"Conditional reflex\",\n      \"Both a & b\",\n      \"Stretched reflex\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The sucking reflex in an infant is innate and present at birth without prior learning, making it an unconditional reflex.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 11,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"In reflex action, reflex arc is formed by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Brain $\\\\rightarrow$ spinal cord $\\\\rightarrow$ muscles\",\n      \"Receptors $\\\\rightarrow$ sensory fibres $\\\\rightarrow$ spinal cord $\\\\rightarrow$ motor fibres $\\\\rightarrow$ effectors\",\n      \"Sensory fibres $\\\\rightarrow$ spinal cord $\\\\rightarrow$ motor fibres $\\\\rightarrow$ receptors $\\\\rightarrow$ effectors\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A classic reflex arc traces from a receptor detecting a stimulus, through sensory nerve fibers to the spinal cord, and out via motor nerve fibers to an effector (muscle or gland).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 12,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cortex of cerebrum has\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Grey matter\",\n      \"White matter\",\n      \"Nerve fibres\",\n      \"All\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The outer layer of the cerebrum, known as the cerebral cortex, is predominantly composed of grey matter (neuronal cell bodies).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 13,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Epithalamus, thalamus and hypothalamus are the parts of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Diencephalon\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Medulla\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The diencephalon serves as a major structural and functional region of the forebrain, consisting of the epithalamus, thalamus, and hypothalamus.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 14,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"\\\"Brain sand\\\" is formed by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Pituitary\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\",\n      \"Pineal gland\",\n      \"Thalamus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Brain sand (corpora arenacea) refers to calcified structures that accumulate over time within the pineal gland.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 15,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Relay centre of brain is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Hypothalamus\",\n      \"Thalamus\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The thalamus acts as the primary sensory relay station, routing incoming sensory signals to the appropriate regions of the cerebral cortex.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 16,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cell bodies of afferent fibres of spinal nerve lie in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"White matter\",\n      \"Dorsal root ganglion\",\n      \"Grey matter\",\n      \"Ventral root\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The cell bodies of incoming sensory (afferent) neurons are clustered in a structure known as the dorsal root ganglion outside the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 17,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Reflex action is quick in comparison to normal action because transmission of normal action occurs through\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum of brain\",\n      \"Entire spinal cord\",\n      \"Olfactory capsules\",\n      \"Pituitary body\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Reflexes are rapid because they typically bypass the conscious decision-making regions (cerebrum) of the brain.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 18,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following prevents the leakage of an impulse in nerve fibres?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Schwann cells\",\n      \"Neurilemma\",\n      \"Axons\",\n      \"Myelin sheath\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator surrounding the axon, reducing ion leakage and increasing conduction velocity.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 19,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Anesthetics reduce pain by blocking nerve conduction due to\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Blocking neurotransmitter receptors\",\n      \"Blocking $\\\\text{Na}^+$ channels\",\n      \"Blocking $\\\\text{K}^+$ channels\",\n      \"All the above\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Local and general anesthetics exert their effects by disrupting various mechanisms of signal propagation, prominently including the blockade of voltage-gated ion channels and neurotransmitter activity.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 20,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Brain is dependent upon blood supply for\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$\\\\text{O}_2$ and ATP\",\n      \"$\\\\text{O}_2$ and electrolytes\",\n      \"$\\\\text{O}_2$ and glucose\",\n      \"ATP and glucose\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The brain has minimal energy reserves and requires a constant blood supply to deliver oxygen and glucose for ATP production.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 21,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which one is a slow neuro-transmitter?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"GABA\",\n      \"Adrenaline\",\n      \"Nor adrenaline\",\n      \"Acetylcholine\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"While neurotransmitters can have varied receptors, GABA often acts via metabotropic receptors in some pathways leading to slower, prolonged modulatory effects compared to the rapid actions of typical fast neurotransmitters.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 22,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"One of the actions of autonomic nervous system is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Pupillary reflex\",\n      \"Knee jerk response\",\n      \"Swallowing of food\",\n      \"Peristalsis of the intestines\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Peristalsis involves involuntary smooth muscle contractions in the digestive tract, strictly governed by the autonomic nervous system.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 23,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Parasympathetic neural system\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Constricts urinary bladder\",\n      \"Relaxes urinary bladder\",\n      \"Inhibits gastric secretion\",\n      \"Accelerates heart beat\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The parasympathetic system promotes 'rest and digest' actions, which includes contracting the detrusor muscle of the urinary bladder to enable urination.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 24,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Longest cranial nerve of head region is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Pathetic\",\n      \"Auditory\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Although vagus is longest overall, the trigeminal nerve is the largest and most extensive cranial nerve restricted predominantly to the head region.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 25,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Hypoglossal nerve controls movement of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Tongue\",\n      \"Heart\",\n      \"Eye\",\n      \"Ear\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The hypoglossal nerve (cranial nerve XII) provides motor innervation to the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 26,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Centre for sense of smell is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Olfactory lobes\",\n      \"Cerebrum\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The olfactory bulbs relay information directly into the olfactory cortex located within the cerebrum for smell processing.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 27,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Arbor vitae is composed of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Grey matter\",\n      \"White matter\",\n      \"Neuroglia cells\",\n      \"All\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The arbor vitae is the highly branched white matter core of the cerebellum, though functionally considered with surrounding tissues. The correct answer key designates it as containing multiple components ('All').\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 28,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The nerve impulse in nerve fibre is conducted when permeability increase mainly for\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sodium ions\",\n      \"Chloride ions\",\n      \"Potassium\",\n      \"Calcium\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A massive temporary increase in membrane permeability to sodium ions initiates depolarization, the first step of an action potential.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 29,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"If the vagus nerve going to heart is stimulated, its heart beat will\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Increase\",\n      \"Decrease\",\n      \"Remains same\",\n      \"Not affected\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Stimulation of the vagus nerve enhances parasympathetic output, which slows the rate of the cardiac pacemaker cells and decreases heart rate.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 30,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Ratio of $\\\\text{Na}^+$ and $\\\\text{K}^+$ ions across nerve membrane is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$50:40$\",\n      \"$60:30$\",\n      \"$30:10$\",\n      \"$30:20$\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The sodium-potassium pump actively transports 3 $\\\\text{Na}^+$ ions out for every 2 $\\\\text{K}^+$ ions brought into the cell, a ratio of 3:2 (or 30:20).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 31,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Adrenaline affects directly\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$\\\\beta$ cells of Langerhans\",\n      \"Epithelial cells of stomach\",\n      \"S.A. node\",\n      \"Dorsal root of spinal nerve\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Adrenaline (epinephrine) binds to adrenergic receptors directly on the S.A. node in the heart, increasing the rate and force of cardiac contractions.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 32,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The anterior choroid plexus is situated in the roof of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$3^{\\\\text{rd}}$ ventricle\",\n      \"$4^{\\\\text{th}}$ ventricle\",\n      \"Lateral ventricle\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The anterior choroid plexus is a secretory network located in the roof of the diencephalon, which corresponds to the third ventricle.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 33,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Aqueduct of Sylvius (Iter) occurs between\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Rhinocoel and lateral ventricles\",\n      \"Third and fourth ventricles\",\n      \"The two lateral ventricles\",\n      \"Lateral ventricles and third ventricles\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius) is a narrow channel through the midbrain that links the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 34,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cerebellum contains\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Lateral ventricles\",\n      \"Cerebellar ventricles\",\n      \"Fourth ventricles\",\n      \"No ventricle\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"While the fourth ventricle separates the cerebellum from the brainstem, the cerebellar hemispheres themselves are solid and contain no internal ventricles.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 35,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Anterior choroid plexus and posterior choroid plexus secrete\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Body fluid\",\n      \"Coelomic fluid\",\n      \"Cerebrospinal fluid\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The principal physiological function of the various choroid plexuses is to produce and secrete cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 36,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Normal glucose level of CSF is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$80-120$ mmHg\",\n      \"$20-40$ mg/dl\",\n      \"$100$ mg/dl\",\n      \"$40-60$ mg/dl\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The normal glucose concentration in cerebrospinal fluid is approximately 60% of blood glucose levels, typically falling in the $40-60$ mg/dL range.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 37,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which nerve does not arise from human medulla oblangata?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"$7^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"$6^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"$10^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"$12^{\\\\text{th}}$\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The $7^{\\\\text{th}}$ cranial nerve (facial nerve) emerges from the brainstem between the pons and the medulla, but primarily at the pontomedullary junction (originating mainly in the pons).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 38,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The lungs, heart, intestine, etc are supplied by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Abducens\",\n      \"Occulomotor\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vagus nerve (CN X) wanders broadly to provide parasympathetic supply to major thoracic and abdominal viscera including the heart, lungs, and intestines.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 39,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Sympathetic neural system\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Promotes glycogen formation\",\n      \"Promotes sugar release\",\n      \"Contracts gall bladder\",\n      \"Dilates arteries\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"During the 'fight or flight' response, the sympathetic nervous system stimulates the liver to break down glycogen into glucose, promoting sugar release for energy.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 40,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Broca's area is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sensory speech area\",\n      \"Motor speech area\",\n      \"Primary sensational area\",\n      \"Autorythemic area\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Broca's area, located in the frontal lobe, coordinates the complex motor sequences required for speech production.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 41,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Injury to vagus nerve will not effect\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Gastro intestinal movement\",\n      \"Tongue movements\",\n      \"Cardiac movements\",\n      \"Pancreatic secretion\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Tongue movements are controlled by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), not the vagus nerve.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 42,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Choroid plexus is network of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Muscle fibres\",\n      \"Lymph capillaries\",\n      \"Blood capillaries\",\n      \"Nerves\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"A choroid plexus consists of a rich network of blood capillaries lined by specialized ependymal cells to filter plasma and form CSF.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 43,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Intercellular communication in multicellular organisms occurs through\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Nervous system only\",\n      \"Digestive system only\",\n      \"Respiratory system only\",\n      \"Both nervous and endocrine system\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Both the rapidly acting nervous system and the slower, hormone-mediated endocrine system are vital for comprehensive intercellular communication and integration.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 44,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"CSF is not present in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Ventricles of brain\",\n      \"Spinal cord (central canal)\",\n      \"Subdural space\",\n      \"Sub-arachnoid space\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Cerebrospinal fluid physically resides in the ventricles, the central canal, and the subarachnoid space, but not naturally in the potential subdural space.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 45,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Nerve cell body of pre-ganglionic fibre of sympathetic nervous system originates from\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Brain\",\n      \"Spinal cord\",\n      \"Sympathetic chain\",\n      \"All\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Sympathetic preganglionic neurons originate strictly from the lateral horns of the grey matter of the thoracic and lumbar segments of the spinal cord.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 46,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Mark the cranial nerves involved in communicating the sense of taste\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Facial and glossopharyngeal\",\n      \"$7^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"$9^{\\\\text{th}}$\",\n      \"All\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Taste sensation is conveyed by the facial nerve (CN VII, anterior tongue), the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX, posterior tongue), making all listed options relevant.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 47,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Which of the following is not a function of parasympathetic nervous system?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"It accelerates peristalsis\",\n      \"It constricts pupil\",\n      \"Contracts urinary bladder\",\n      \"Increase rate of heart beat\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The parasympathetic nervous system decreases heart rate; increasing heart rate is a sympathetic function.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 48,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Unipolar nerve is found in which cranial nerve?\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Olfactory\",\n      \"Trochlear\",\n      \"Trigeminal\",\n      \"Hypoglossal\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Unique among cranial nerves, sensory neurons whose cell bodies lie in the mesencephalic nucleus of the trigeminal nerve are functionally unipolar (pseudounipolar).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 49,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Highly vascular and closely investing protective coat around brain is known as\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Arachnoid\",\n      \"Piamater\",\n      \"Duramater\",\n      \"Sub-arachnoid space\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The pia mater is the innermost meningeal layer that adheres tightly to the surface of the brain and is richly supplied with blood vessels.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 50,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"One of the following is not the lobe of cerebral hemisphere\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Parietal lobe\",\n      \"Occipital lobe\",\n      \"Temporal lobe\",\n      \"Olfactory lobe\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"While olfactory bulbs are forebrain structures, the four major anatomical lobes of the cerebral hemispheres are the frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 51,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Characteristically large flask shaped Purkinje cells are associated with\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebral cortex\",\n      \"Cerebellar cortex\",\n      \"Pons\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Purkinje cells are huge, extensively branched neurons found uniquely in the cortex of the cerebellum.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 52,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The CSF moves from ventricle of the brain to subarachnoid space through\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Foramina Magendie\",\n      \"Foramina Luschka\",\n      \"Foramen of Monro\",\n      \"Both a & b\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"CSF exits the fourth ventricle and enters the subarachnoid space via the midline foramen of Magendie and the two lateral foramina of Luschka.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 53,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The pneumotaxis centre in the body is present in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Heart\",\n      \"Lungs\",\n      \"Pons varolli\",\n      \"Medulla\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The pneumotaxic center, which fine-tunes respiratory rate and depth, is located in the upper portion of the pons.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 54,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The neurotransmitter with the sympathetic post-ganglionic nerve fibre terminating at the sweat gland is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Epinephrine\",\n      \"Acetylcholine\",\n      \"Adrenaline\",\n      \"Both a & c\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"As an exception to typical sympathetic adrenergic pathways, the sympathetic fibers innervating most eccrine sweat glands release acetylcholine.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 55,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Nerve related to eyeball movement, accommodation and contraction of pupil is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Optic\",\n      \"Auditory\",\n      \"Oculomotor\",\n      \"Trochlear\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The oculomotor nerve (CN III) innervates most of the extraocular muscles for eye movement and provides parasympathetic control for pupillary constriction and lens accommodation.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 56,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Pallium is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Lateral walls of diencephalon\",\n      \"Lateral walls of cerebrum\",\n      \"Floor walls of paracoel\",\n      \"Roof of paracoel\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"In comparative vertebrate anatomy, the pallium refers to the thick roof of the cerebrum (paracoel).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 57,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The two lobes of cerebellum are connected by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Corpus callosum\",\n      \"Vermis\",\n      \"Foramen of Monro\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vermis is the narrow, worm-like midline structure that joins the two lateral cerebellar hemispheres.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 58,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Those nerves which carry impulses from the CNS to the muscles and glands are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sensory nerves\",\n      \"Motor nerves\",\n      \"Mixed nerves\",\n      \"Afferent nerves\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Motor (efferent) nerves transmit command impulses outwards from the central nervous system to effectors like muscles and glands.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 59,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Branched tree like structure present in cerebellum is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Arborial\",\n      \"Areole\",\n      \"Arbor vitae\",\n      \"Archenteron\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The arbor vitae (Latin for 'tree of life') is the tree-like branching of white matter seen in a cross-section of the cerebellum.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 60,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Drinking of alcohol affects\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Medulla\",\n      \"Diencephalon\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Alcohol intoxication rapidly impairs the functioning of the cerebellum, resulting in ataxia, poor coordination, and loss of balance.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 61,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Somaesthetic area is present in ---- of cerebrum\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Frontal lobe\",\n      \"Temporal lobe\",\n      \"Parietal lobe\",\n      \"Occipital lobe\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The primary somatosensory (somaesthetic) cortex is located in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 62,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Musician's nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Axillary\",\n      \"Median\",\n      \"Ulnar\",\n      \"Radial\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The ulnar nerve is colloquially referred to as the musician's nerve because it controls fine movements of the fingers critical for playing instruments.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 63,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Labourer's nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Median\",\n      \"Ulnar\",\n      \"Radial\",\n      \"Sciatic\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The median nerve is often termed the labourer's nerve because it provides coarse grip and forearm pronation necessary for physical labor.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 64,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cranial nerve which controls the sound production is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Facial\",\n      \"Trochlear\",\n      \"Vagus\",\n      \"Hypoglossal\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vagus nerve supplies motor branches to the muscles of the larynx, thus directly controlling phonation (sound production).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 65,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Smallest cranial nerve is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Trochlear\",\n      \"Olfactory\",\n      \"Optic\",\n      \"Auditory\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The trochlear nerve (CN IV) innervates only a single eye muscle and contains the smallest number of axons among all cranial nerves.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 66,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Jumping of action potential at the nodes of Ranvier is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Recovery phase\",\n      \"Active phase\",\n      \"Saltatory conduction\",\n      \"Neurotransmission\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Saltatory conduction is the process whereby action potentials leap rapidly from one node of Ranvier to the next along myelinated axons.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 67,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Brain ventricles are lined by\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Neurons\",\n      \"Schwann cells\",\n      \"Neuroglia\",\n      \"Ependymal cells\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The ventricles of the brain are internally lined by an epithelial-like layer of neuroglial cells called ependymal cells.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 68,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Daily rhythms are usually associated with\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Thymus\",\n      \"Pituitary\",\n      \"Pineal\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The pineal gland secretes melatonin, a hormone fundamentally involved in synchronizing circadian (daily) rhythms.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 69,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Post ganglionic sympathetic fibres are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Adrenergic\",\n      \"Cholinergic\",\n      \"Both\",\n      \"None\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vast majority of postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine and are therefore classified as adrenergic.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 70,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Cholesterol content is maximum in\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Gray matter\",\n      \"White matter\",\n      \"Kidney\",\n      \"Liver\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"White matter is dense with myelin sheaths, which are exceptionally rich in lipids, especially cholesterol.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 71,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Epidural space of spinal cord contains\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Fatty tissue\",\n      \"Connective tissue\",\n      \"Veins\",\n      \"All\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The epidural space outside the spinal dura mater contains fat, loose connective tissue, and a venous plexus.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 72,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"In the brain of mammals, the genu and splenium are associated with\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebrum\",\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Medulla\",\n      \"Vermis\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The genu (anterior bend) and splenium (posterior end) are specific structural regions of the corpus callosum within the cerebrum.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 73,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"Grey matter is composed of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Myelin\",\n      \"Cell bodies of neuron\",\n      \"Dendrites\",\n      \"Axons\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\n    \"solutionText\": \"Grey matter consists mainly of neuronal cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons, giving it its greyish hue.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 74,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"White matter is composed of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Nerve fibres\",\n      \"Nerve cells\",\n      \"Ependyma\",\n      \"None of these\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"White matter is predominantly composed of bundles of myelinated nerve fibers (axons) carrying signals between brain regions.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 75,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"CNS is mostly made of\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Sensory & motor neurons\",\n      \"Sensory and Association neurons\",\n      \"Association neurons\",\n      \"Motor and Association neurons\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The vast majority of neurons in the brain and spinal cord are interneurons (association neurons) that process and relay information.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 76,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"A person feels no sensation when he puts this hand over flame; the part of the brain which has damaged is\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Cerebellum\",\n      \"Medulla oblongata\",\n      \"Diencephalon\",\n      \"Hypothalamus\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"The thalamus, located in the diencephalon, is the critical relay center for all somatosensory pathways (except smell). Damage here blocks sensory perception.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 77,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The supporting & nutritive cells found in the brain are\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Microglia\",\n      \"Astrocytes\",\n      \"Ependymal cells\",\n      \"Oligo dendrocytes\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\n    \"solutionText\": \"According to the provided answer key, ependymal cells (which line the ventricles and help produce nutritive CSF) are the correct option for this question.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"questionOrder\": 78,\n    \"questionType\": \"Objective\",\n    \"questionText\": \"The function of cerebrospinal fluid does not include\",\n    \"questionOptions\":[\n      \"Protection of brain & spinal cord by containing\",\n      \"Protection of delicate brain and spinal cord from shock\",\n      \"As a medium for excretion of waste product\",\n      \"Buoyancy to brain\"\n    ],\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\n    \"solutionText\": \"CSF provides shock absorption, buoyancy, and waste excretion. Option 'a' is either nonsensical or incomplete, meaning it is not a correct function.\"\n  }\n]\n"
